   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law and the state finance law, in relation to\nallocating fines imposed on real estate appraisers to the anti-discrimination\nin housing fund",
    "basePrintNo": "S8874",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8874-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§160-u, 160-e, 160-v & 160-w, Exec L; amd §80-a, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8874\n\nSPONSOR: REICHLIN-MELNICK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law and the state finance law, in relation\nto allocating fines imposed on real estate appraisers to the anti-dis-\ncrimination in housing fund\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo address appraisal discrimination by imposing a fine for violation of\nstate and federal fair housing policies and laws, and having those fines\nallocated to the anti-discrimination in housing fund.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the opening paragraph of subdivision 1 of section\n160-u of the executive law, reads that state-certified licensed real\nestate appraisers may have their certification or license revoked,\nsuspended, fined not exceeding two thousand dollars, or disciplined\nbased on the provision of this article. Fifty percent of any money\nreceived in fines by the department of state shall be allocated to the\n\"anti-discrimination in housing fund\" established by section 80-a of the\nstate finance law. The department may revoke, suspend, fine, or disci-\npline a state certified or licensed real estate appraiser for reasons\nlisted in section two.\n\nSection two, three, and four amend various subdivisions of section 160\nof the executive law to permit that based on disciplinary proceedings,\ncertificates or licenses can be suspended, revoked, or fines imposed on\nreal estate appraisers, provided that appropriate written notice and\nhearings be provided. If the department determines that a state certi-\nfied or licensed appraiser has violated any provision in the article the\ndepartment shall investigate and recommend that the appraiser be repri-\nmanded or have their certification or license suspended, revoked, or\nfined.\n\nSection five amends subdivisions 2 and 3 of section 80-a of the state\nfinance law, that the anti-discrimination in housing fund dhsll be fund-\ned by fines and forfeitures provided under various sections of law. The\nstate can also fund the anti-discrimination in housing fund with grants,\ngifts, or bequests. Money in the anti-discrimination fund shall not be\ncommingled with other money in the custody of the state comptroller, and\nthis fund shall be available to use by the office of the attorney gener-\nal, for fair housing testing, including for testing appraisers. Also,\nfunds shall be allocated to county, city, town, or village human rights\ncommissions, or other non-profit agencies that specialize in the\nprevention of unlawful discrimination in housing.\n\nSection 6 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe United States has a long history of excluding Black Americans and\nother marginalized groups from fully participating in American society,\nand from wealth-building activities. The New Deal created the Federal\nHousing Administration (FHA) and the organization provided mortgage\ninsurance on loans made by FHA-approved lenders.  This mechanism\nprotected lenders against losses, enticing more lenders to take the risk\nand provide loans to a wide variety of people. The FHA intentionally\ncreated policies that limited lending to white communities and excluded\nBlack and Hispanic communities. The federal government designated the\nmere presence of Black, Hispanic, and other ethnic minority groups as an\neconomic hazard and color-coded these neighborhoods 'red' on federal\nmaps, a hazardous zone. These 'redlined' communities were unable to\nparticipate in the largest wealth accumulation process of the 20th\ncentury, the growth of the housing market following the Second World\nWar.\n\nAnother exclusionary aspect of federal policies was the limiting of\nfederal funds to developers. To access federal funding, housing develop-\ners were encouraged to have racial covenants forbidding non-whites from\npurchasing homes. During the height of FHA's discriminatory practices,\nBlack homeownership rate was 35%. Although the federal government ended\nits explicitly racist policy with the passing of the 1968 Fair Housing\nAct, nothing in the act addressed the past harm done; as of 2021 Black\nhomeownership rate is at 44% and white homeownership rate is 74%.\n\nMost Americans build their wealth by growing their home's equity, howev-\ner, these racist policies limited the ability of Black and Hispanic\nAmericans' ability to build their wealth. Today many people of color,\nand particularly Black Americans continue to face an uphill battle to\nincrease their home equity; the main wealth-building process in the\ncountry.\n\nMany Black Americans must still contend with appraisal discrimination,\nwhich unfairly devalues their homes. In 2018 a Brookings Institute\nreport found that homes in a majority Black neighborhood, even when\ncontrolling for the homes' physical characteristics and neighborhood\namenities, were undervalued by 23% an average of $48,000 per home, a\ncumulative loss of $156 billion in total wealth for Black Americans in\n2016. The authors also found that devalued homes in Black neighborhoods\nwere more segregated, and that Black children who grew up in these\ncommunities had less upward mobility. The devaluation of Black homes\nalso has a negative impact on community schools.\n\nThe Fair Housing Act was amended in 1988 to include appraising as a real\nestate-related transaction. However, this does not prevent appraisers\nfrom producing biased appraisals. The federal government has taken\naction to address appraisal discrimination with the creation of the\nProperty Appraisal Valuation Equity (PAVE) task force in June of 2021.\nThe PAVE task force recently completed its evaluation and has compiled a\nset of possible solutions, but absent action at the federal level, there\nare actions that can be taken at the state level today.\n\nRacial and ethnic minority groups have historically been excluded from\nwealth-building activities, and appraisal discrimination is a continua-\ntion of this legacy. While appraisal discrimination is embedded in the\nhome purchasing process there are several actions that can be taken at\nthe state level to correct or reduce the harm caused by federal and\nstate policies. New York can end the trauma that many Black New Yorkers\nexperience when they realize that their very presence, their existence,\nis devaluing their homes. This legislation will help bring an end to\nappraisal discrimination and give all residents of NY a chance to fully\nand fairly participate in the housing market.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew legislation.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided that if chapter 687 of\nthe laws of 2021 shall not have taken effect on or before such date then\nsection four of this act shall take effect on the same date and in the\nsame manner as such chapter of the laws of 2021 takes effect.\n",
